# HTML

Write clear, semantic, accessible markup from the first draft — not a monolith
page to clean up later.

## Docs & comments

Document purpose clearly; comment intent — never narrate obvious tags.
Inline/`<!-- -->` comments follow `global-code-comments` (default sparse).

- Every HTML document starts with a short top-of-file comment describing the
  page’s purpose
- Section landmarks and non-obvious blocks get a brief comment when the
  structure isn’t clear from headings alone

```html
<!-- dashboard.html — Operator overview: status summary and recent jobs. -->

<main>
  <!-- Live status cards; values filled by dashboard.js -->
  <section aria-labelledby="status-heading">
    ...
  </section>
</main>
```

## Structure & semantics

- Prefer semantic elements (`main`, `nav`, `header`, `footer`, `section`,
  `article`, `button`, `label`) over generic `<div>` / `<span>` soup
- Give elements meaningful `id`s when practical (hooks for JS, CSS, tests, and
  deep links) — stable, purpose-based, unique in the document
- One page, one `<h1>`; heading levels nest without skipping
- When a page grows, split into partials/templates/components using whatever
  the project already uses
- Keep scripts and styles out of the markup when the project has external
  `.js` / `.css`
- Be reasonable: don’t invent a component framework for a static two-section
  page

## Accessibility & forms

- Interactive controls must be real `<button>`, `<a href>`, or form fields —
  not clickable `<div>`s
- Every form input has an associated `<label>` (or otherwise valid accessible
  name)
- Images that convey meaning get meaningful `alt`; decorative images get
  `alt=""`
- Prefer native semantics before ARIA roles
- Preserve keyboard focus order; don’t remove focus outlines without an
  accessible replacement

## Robustness & safety

- Produce valid HTML5; treat nesting/closing errors like type errors
- Never place secrets, tokens, or credentials in HTML
- Escape/encode untrusted content; do not inject raw user HTML unless the
  project has a deliberate, sanitized pipeline
- Prefer external scripts with clear `type`/`defer`/`async` per project
  convention over large inline script blocks
- When behavior is non-trivial, pair the page with JS modules that follow
  `global-javascript` (tested there — not with ad-hoc inline scripts)
